meta {
  name: rate-dish-correction
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

put {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/guest/v1/orders/{{order_uid}}/items/{{order_item_uid}}/review
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-Guest-Token: {{guest_token}}
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "rating": 4,
    "tags": ["good_portion"],
    "comment": "Lovely, just a little rich for me."
  }
}

tests {
  test("responds 200", function () {
    expect(res.status).to.equal(200)
  })

  test("updates the existing review rather than adding a second", function () {
    // Same uid as rate-dish returned. One review per order line, enforced by a unique index --
    // which is also what keeps the dish's rating_count and rating_sum on menu_item reconcilable
    // against this table.
    expect(res.body.data.uid).to.equal(bru.getVar("review_uid"))
  })

  test("the correction replaced the rating, it did not average with it", function () {
    expect(res.body.data.rating).to.equal(4)
  })

  test("tags are replaced wholesale, not merged", function () {
    // "tasty" and "worth_the_wait" from the first call are gone. A diner correcting their mind
    // is stating a new opinion, not appending to the old one.
    expect(res.body.data.tags).to.deep.equal(["good_portion"])
  })

  test("the optional comment is stored", function () {
    expect(res.body.data.comment).to.equal("Lovely, just a little rich for me.")
  })
}

docs {
  # Correct a rating

The same endpoint as `rate-dish`, run a second time on the same line. This is what a diner
re-tapping a different star does.

The dish's aggregate on `menu_item` moves by the DIFFERENCE (here 5 -> 4, so the sum drops by one
and the count does not move), rather than being recomputed. That arithmetic happens in SQL inside
the same transaction as the review write: a read-modify-write in Go would let two diners rating the
same dish in the same second each read the same starting value, and one rating would silently
vanish.
}
